The random control comparison shows that 17 of 19 random-gene controls achieved silhouette scores $\ge$ 0.099 (the metabolic clustering), corresponding to an add-one rank fraction of 0.90. This indicates that the observed clustering is more similar to random clustering than to HVG-based clustering. The CD8 T cell subset clustering metrics are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:cd8_metrics}.

\subsection{Stability and Reproducibility}
Clustering stability was assessed via Leiden randomization on a fixed PCA/neighbor graph. The mean pairwise ARI across seeds was 0.759 (minimum 0.598), indicating moderate reproducibility. The resolution selection used a stability filter of \texttt{mean\_pairwise\_ari} $\ge$ 0.75, with the tie-breaker being highest mean pairwise ARI.

\subsection{Confounder Assessment}
We checked for confounding by patient, sample, source, cell cycle phase, complexity, total counts, detected genes, and mitochondrial percentage. The categorical NMI for patient was 0.393, for sample 0.443, for source 0.347, and for cell cycle phase 0.274. Within-patient (replicate) analysis showed varying degrees of cluster composition overlap across 3 patients, with patient 6 being the weakest replicate (2,776 cells). The global-vs-within ARI range for the CD8 subset was 0.511 to 0.845.

\subsection{Candidate Partitions Interpretation}
The observed 6 candidate clusters within CD8 T cells show some degree of separation, but the conclusion is \textbf{inconclusive} regarding distinct transcriptional metabolic states. The weaker silhouette score (0.099) and high random control exceedance (17/19) suggest that the clustering is not strongly supported by the metabolic gene signal. The UMAP visualization in Figure~\ref{fig:cd8_umap} shows the spatial distribution of candidate clusters, while Figure~\ref{fig:cd8_diagnostics} displays clustering quality metrics and random control comparisons.

